# Response headers for every path served by the Worker's static assets.
#
# THESE LIVE HERE AND NOT IN THE DASHBOARD because a file in the tree can be
# diffed, guarded (section 104) and shipped inside a release, and a dashboard
# rule can be none of those. Dashboard Transform Rules DO apply to these
# responses and they WIN — one served a stale Permissions-Policy for a whole
# release while every guard stayed green — so the wire is read after every
# deploy (RELEASING.md, "The wire checks"); no guard can read a panel, and one
# that pretended to would be worse than an honest gap.
#
# This file applies to responses the assets plane serves. The two responses
# worker.js constructs restate the security set themselves; guard 133 holds
# them equal to the /* block below.
#
# HSTS and X-Content-Type-Options are set at the edge instead (SSL/TLS panel
# and Managed Transforms) and are deliberately not duplicated here.
#
# Content-Security-Policy stays in the <meta> tag in index.html: the guards
# bless its hash against the one inline script, and moving it to a header
# would split one rule across two files. A response-header scan cannot see a
# <meta> policy and will report it missing; the header such a scan proposes
# ('unsafe-inline') is weaker than the single sha256 that ships.
#
# CACHE POLICY. Nothing declared one before 3.0.0, so every file was cached
# with whatever Workers Assets emits by default — and the bare /sw.js once
# answered a version two releases old while the same file with a cache-busting
# query answered the current one. sw.js is how a returning browser learns the
# app changed, so it is no-cache (revalidate before use, not "do not store");
# so is / , because the document IS the app and a non-SW visitor must
# revalidate it or a bad deploy is sticky for exactly the readers the service
# worker cannot help. The fonts are content-addressed by the subset script's
# own hash record and never change under a name, so they take the year with
# immutable. The icons, the manifest and the share card take A DAY, not a
# year: a year with immutable is only honest behind a content-hashed filename,
# and "unstable or frequently changed" is a listed Google cause of favicon
# non-appearance, so the names stay stable and the policy stays short. If the
# names ever become hashed they move to the fonts' policy.
#
# Permissions-Policy denies usb=() because the app never uses WebUSB — cheap
# hardening. interest-cohort is NOT listed: FLoC was withdrawn, and section
# 104 refuses that token by name.
#
# THE Link: LINES SIT UNDER /, NOT /*. A canonical relation is a statement
# about a document; /* would apply it to fonts, icons and sw.js, and RFC 8288
# gives no reading of "this PNG's authoritative version is the home page".
# _headers matches on PATH ONLY — never content type — and every matching rule
# applies cumulatively with no way to unset, so scoping means declaring under
# / rather than /*. / is the whole HTML surface: wrangler.jsonc pins
# not_found_handling to "404-page", so no other path ever serves a document.
# describedby is the honest registered relation for an agent — the site has
# no API, so RFC 9727's api-catalog and service-doc would be fabrication — and
# /llms.txt IS the machine-readable description of this page, the same file
# the Worker serves to any client whose Accept prefers text/markdown. The
# target is site-relative where the other two are absolute: canonical and
# sitemap name authoritative URLs and must not drift across origins; a
# describedby points at a sibling of whatever origin served it.
#
# Vary: Accept on / because worker.js negotiates the root: the HTML
# fall-through is a second representation of the same URL, and a shared cache
# that relaxes no-cache revalidation could otherwise hand HTML to a
# markdown-preferring agent. The negotiation is in worker.js; the declaration
# belongs in the file that can be diffed and guarded.
#
# The files written for agents (auth.md, llms.txt, orders.txt) and the
# disclosure pointer (security.txt, which carries an Expires date) are
# no-cache for the same reason the page is: an agent or a scanner asks once,
# writes the answer down, and does not come back to see whether it changed.
# Nothing here declares Content-Type: the assets plane already emits the right
# one for .txt, and _headers cannot unset a header it duplicates.
#
# The history of every decision above — dates, releases, the evidence files —
# is in NOTES.md ("Where the served and config files' histories went").

/*
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin

/
  Link: <https://nightwatcher.life/sitemap.xml>; rel="sitemap"
  Link: <https://nightwatcher.life/>; rel="canonical"
  Link: </llms.txt>; rel="describedby"
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Vary: Accept

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/auth.md
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/llms.txt
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/orders.txt
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/.well-known/security.txt
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/icon.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon-16x16.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon-32x32.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon-48x48.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/mstile-144x144.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/icon-192.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/icon-maskable-512.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/share.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
